+Color/IR choice, displayed coordinate system, zoom, and pan remain view-local.
+Each ordinary view exposes **Scan coordinates** and **Screen coordinates** in
+its toolbar. The latter is libcolorscreen's `final` coordinate system: the
+geometrically corrected screen plane also used by screen-geometry file output
+and stitched projects. Scan mode preserves the existing Qt crop/rotation/mirror
+presentation; Screen mode lays out the canvas in final coordinates while mouse
+tools, registration edits, crop/measurement callbacks, and panel APIs continue
+to exchange scan coordinates for ordinary single scans. The public tile API
+selects the same distinction with `render_scan_coordinates` or
+`render_final_coordinates`; the legacy `render_tile` overload remains scan-
+coordinate behavior. Stitched projects have no single scan coordinate system
+and therefore use final coordinates. Slanted-edge reference views remain
+intentionally specialized and keep their separate Navigation + Sharpness-only
+inspector in scan coordinates.
 
 Top-level menus follow the conventional order **File, Edit, View,
 Registration, Window, Help**. `Window` is the final working menu and owns MDI
